WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.594
Book Four, Proposition Twelve.

2
00:00:02.594 --> 00:00:08.173
About a given circle to circumscribe an equilateral and equiangular pentagon.

3
00:00:08.173 --> 00:00:17.810
Let A B C D E be the given circle; it is required to circumscribe an equilateral and equiangular pentagon about the circle A B C D E.

4
00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:31.365
By Four Eleven, let A, B, C, D, and E be the vertices of an inscribed regular pentagon, so that the arcs A B, B C, C D, D E, and E A are equal.

5
00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:41.653
Through A, B, C, D, and E draw G H, H K, K L, L M, and M G tangent to the circle by the Porism to Three Sixteen.

6
00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:49.920
By Three One, take the center F of circle A B C D E, and join F B, F K, F C, F L, and F D.

7
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:59.903
By Three Eighteen, each radius to a point of contact is perpendicular to its tangent, so the angles at B, C, and D are right angles.

8
00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:10.335
In right triangles F B K and F C K, by One Forty-Seven the square on F K equals the squares on F C and C K, and also the squares on F B and B K.

9
00:01:10.335 --> 00:01:17.652
Since radii F C and F B are equal, the remaining squares on C K and B K are equal, so B K equals C K.

10
00:01:17.652 --> 00:01:34.169
By One Eight, triangles F B K and F C K are congruent, making central angle B F C double angle K F C, and corner angle B K C double angle F K C; similarly angle C F D is double angle C F L, and angle D L C is double angle F L C.

11
00:01:34.169 --> 00:01:44.528
Because arc B C equals arc C D, by Three Twenty-Seven central angle B F C equals angle C F D, so their halves—angles K F C and L F C—are equal.

12
00:01:44.528 --> 00:01:58.003
Since right angles F C K and F C L are also equal and side F C is common, by One Twenty-Six triangles F K C and F L C are congruent, so K C equals C L and angle F K C equals angle F L C.

13
00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:04.378
Because K C equals C L, tangent side K L is double K C, and similarly H K is double B K.

14
00:02:04.378 --> 00:02:13.216
Since B K equals K C, side H K equals K L, and by the same reasoning all five sides G H, H K, K L, L M, and M G are equal.

15
00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:24.590
Moreover, since angle F K C equals angle F L C, their doubles—interior angles H K L and K L M—are equal, and all five angles of pentagon G H K L M are equal.

16
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:34.660
Therefore an equilateral and equiangular pentagon G H K L M has been circumscribed about the given circle A B C D E, as was required to do.

17
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.790
Proposition Four Twelve establishes Euclid's universal dualization method for regular polygons.

18
00:02:40.790 --> 00:03:02.069
As De Morgan and Heath observe, once any regular polygon is inscribed in a circle, drawing tangents at its vertices and pairing the radial right triangles via the Pythagorean Theorem and Angle-Side-Angle congruence circumscribes a regular polygon of the same number of sides—a general principle Euclid explicitly invokes in Four Fifteen and Four Sixteen.

19
00:03:02.069 --> 00:03:19.320
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-four synthesizes fifteen direct prerequisites across one hundred ten ancestor nodes, uniting the Pythagorean Theorem, SSS and ASA congruence, circle center-finding, and tangent perpendicularity with the inscribed pentagon of Four Eleven.

